## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic synchronization, simple data structures, and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly way while touching practical OS primitives.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffer and wraparound
- Atomic operations and memory ordering (acquire/release)
- Single-producer/single-consumer vs multiple producers/consumers
- Race conditions and simple mitigation with per-slot flags

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal SP/SC enqueue skeleton
#include <stdatomic.h>
#define N 128
typedef struct { int data; } item_t;
typedef struct { item_t buf[N]; _Atomic size_t head, tail; } queue_t;
int enqueue(queue_t *q, item_t it){
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&q->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if ((h + 1) % N == t) return -1; // full
  q->buf[h % N] = it;
  atomic_store_explicit(&q->head, h+1, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of user-space vs kernel-space concept interplay, synchronization primitives, and designing a lazy I/O cache with proper wakeups.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block state machine (EMPTY, PENDING, LOADED, ERROR)
- Fast path vs slow path with contention handling
- Wait queues (futex/condvar) and wakeup semantics
- Avoiding thundering herd with CAS and double-checked locking
- Error propagation from I/O to callers

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  void *data;
  atomic_int state; // 0=EMPTY,1=PENDING,2=LOADED,3=ERROR
  int waiters;
  pthread_mutex_t mutex;
  pthread_cond_t cond;
} cache_entry;

#define EMPTY 0
#define PENDING 1
#define LOADED 2
#define ERROR 3

void* block_read(int block_id) {
  cache_entry *entry = &cache[block_id];

  // Fast path
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    return entry->data;
  }

  pthread_mutex_lock(&entry->mutex);

  // Double-check after acquiring lock
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);
    return entry->data;
  }

  // Transition to PENDING if first thread
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&entry->state, &EMPTY, PENDING)) {
    enqueue_io_request(block_id);
  }

  // Block until I/O completes
  while (atomic_load(&entry->state) == PENDING) {
    entry->waiters++;
    pthread_cond_wait(&entry->cond, &entry->mutex);
    entry->waiters--;
  }

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);

  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == ERROR) {
    return NULL; // Propagate error
  }

  return entry->data;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical IPC path design, per-core locality optimization, and safe buffer migration under memory pressure in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Per-core allocator**: Each CPU core maintains a local free-list of buffers to minimize cache-line bouncing and lock contention
- **Lock-free CAS synchronization**: Compare-and-swap operations enable atomic updates without global locks
- **Cross-core buffer migration**: Moving buffers between cores to balance load while maintaining locality
- **Quotas and fairness**: Ensuring no single client monopolizes buffer resources

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch (C-like) for acquire_buffer and migrate_buffer
typedef struct buf { 
    int id; 
    int state; 
    struct buf *next; 
} buf_t;

typedef struct core_pool { 
    buf_t *free; 
    atomic_int quota; 
} core_pool_t;

buf_t *acquire_buffer(int core) {
    core_pool_t *p = &pools[core];
    buf_t *b;
    do {
        b = atomic_load(&p->free);
        if (!b) return NULL;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->free, &b, b->next));
    atomic_fetch_sub(&p->quota, 1);
    return b;
}

void migrate_buffer(buf_t *b, int to_core) {
    core_pool_t *p = &pools[to_core];
    buf_t *old_head;
    do {
        old_head = atomic_load(&p->free);
        b->next = old_head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->free, &old_head, b));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
