## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical synchronization primitives in a kernel-like setting, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Wait queues, locks, memory ordering
- Spurious wakeups and rechecking condition
- Correct wakeup under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct waiter { struct waiter *next; task_t *t; } waiter_t;

typedef struct wq { spinlock_t lock; waiter_t *head; bool *flag; } wq_t;

void wait_on(wq_t *q, bool *flag){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  while (!*flag){
    enqueue(q, current); // add to wait list
    release(&q->lock);
    schedule();
    acquire(&q->lock);
  }
  release(&q->lock);
}

void wake_up(wq_t *q){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  *q->flag = true;
  wake_all(q); // wake all waiters
  release(&q->lock);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to handle timeouts?
- How to prevent missed wakeups when wake happens before wait is enqueued?
- Compare to condition variables vs. spin-wait approaches.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about live-code updates in a running kernel, including synchronization, cache coherence, and control of instruction fetch paths under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Live patching of executable memory without stopping workers
- Per-page state machines and atomic publish
- I-cache/TLB synchronization and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (pseudo-code)
enum { STABLE, STAGED, ACTIVE } state_t;
typedef struct { void *addr; void *code; state_t s; volatile int refcnt; uint64_t ver; } page_t;

int publish_patch(page_t *p, void *new_code) {
  p->s = STAGED;
  p->code = new_code;
  __sync_synchronize();
  if (atomic_compare_exchange(&p->s, STAGED, ACTIVE)) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&p->ver, 1);
    icache_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    tlb_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    return 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

