## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a simple physical allocator, reason about fragmentation, testability, and correctness in a beginner-friendly way; uses fundamental data structures; demonstrates thought about coalescing.

## Key Concepts
- Free-list data structure for fixed resources
- Coalescing contiguous free blocks
- Fragmentation metrics and simple tests
- Straightforward, single-threaded correctness assumptions

## Code Example
```c
#define NR_FRAMES 256
typedef struct Frame {
  int next;
  int free;
} Frame;
Frame frames[NR_FRAMES];
int free_head;

void init_allocator() {
  for (int i = 0; i < NR_FRAMES; ++i) {
    frames[i].next = i+1;
    frames[i].free = 1;
  }
  frames[NR_FRAMES-1].next = -1;
  free_head = 0;
}

int alloc_one() {
  if (free_head == -1) return -1;
  int f = free_head;
  free_head = frames[f].next;
  frames[f].free = 0;
  return f;
}

void free_block(int start, int count) {
  int end = start + count - 1;
  frames[end].next = free_head;
  free_head = start;
  for (int i = start; i <= end; ++i) frames[i].free = 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-threaded allocators and introduce synchronization?
- What fragmentation metrics would you track, and how would you prove improvement with coalescing?

## Why This Is Asked
Interacting workers faulting the same page risks double work and stale data; userfaultfd paths require precise coordination to avoid data races and ensure memory visibility across cores. The scenario tests low-level synchronization, memory ordering, and fault handling.

## Key Concepts
- Per-page state machine (UNLOADED, LOADING, READY)
- Atomic CAS and release/acquire semantics
- Futex or condition variable waiting for other threads
- Correct interaction with userfaultfd and page fault semantics
- Performance trade-offs of fine-grained locking vs batching

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo C: per-page loading protocol
typedef enum { UNLOADED=0, LOADING=1, READY=2 } page_state_t;
typedef struct { _Atomic int state; /* 0/1/2 */ } page_t;

void access_page(page_t *p, void *dst) {
  int s = atomic_load_explicit(&p->state, memory_order_acquire);
  if (s == READY) { /* copy data to dst from page */ return; }
  if (s == UNLOADED) {
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&p->state, &UNLOADED, LOADING)) {
      // fault will be resolved by loader
      trigger_fault_and_load(p);
      atomic_store_explicit(&p->state, READY, memory_order_release);
      wake_waiters(p);
      /* copy data to dst */
      return;
    }
  }
  // wait until READY
  wait_on_futex(&p->state, READY);
  /* copy data */
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to NUMA and cache-line alignment?
- What failure modes exist if loader crashes or out-of-memory occurs?

